WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:01.490
- As you may have heard,

00:00:01.490 --> 00:00:04.890
we are in a bit of a housing
crisis here in America.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.840
Median home prices and rental rates

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.120
have been skyrocketing
across much of the country.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.100
If you happen to already own a home,

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.320
congrats! You've probably seen
your equity get a good bump

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.560
in the last few years.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.200
- But if you're like the
millions of Americans who rent

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.790
or are trying to get
into the housing market,

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.900
this, for lack of a better word, sucks.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.610
- This surge in home prices,

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.460
as we explained in an earlier video,

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:31.950
is mainly the result of
having too few homes for sale.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.290
New construction never really recovered

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.480
from the 2008 market collapse.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.180
And that, coupled with strict zoning laws

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.440
and pandemic-fueled shortages
of supplies and labor,

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.450
has created a drastic housing shortage.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.580
- But this problem is exacerbated

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.670
by the unprecedented rise
in real estate speculation.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.930
The housing market is so hot
that it's attracting investors

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.770
across the economic spectrum,

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.770
from mom and pop landlords,

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.070
to huge private equity firms,

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:03.278
It's driving prices even higher

00:01:03.278 --> 00:01:05.480
and making it harder for ordinary families

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.120
to get in the game.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.787
(upbeat music)

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.670
- The last time we had a housing
boom, it didn't end well.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.660
The bubble burst in 2008,

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.400
largely because of subprime mortgages,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.740
poorly structured loans
given out by reckless lenders

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.120
to borrowers who could not afford them.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.960
But recent findings suggest
that real estate investors

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.320
played a greater role than
previously understood.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.930
- According to a study by
three professors of finance,

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:40.840
areas that had a lot
of housing speculation

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.540
saw prices climb higher during the boom

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.890
and plummet lower during the bust.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.450
This had a ripple effect
throughout the economy.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:49.983
Unemployment, wage stagnation,

00:01:49.983 --> 00:01:52.340
even closures of local businesses

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:54.250
were all worse in areas

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.180
that had a high level
of housing speculation.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.110
- To be clear, real estate investment

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.510
did not cause the Great Recession,

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.657
but it seems to have
exacerbated the damage.

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:06.710
"In the absence of speculation,

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:08.490
the size of the boom and bust

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.900
would have been about a third smaller,"

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.850
said one author of the study.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.100
- Why did this happen?

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.640
Well, if a lot of speculators
descend on a certain area,

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.680
there are fewer houses to go around,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.260
which drives up prices,

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:24.220
but no one's actually
living in these houses.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:25.970
So when the market turns south,

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.630
you end up with too much
inventory, pushing prices lower.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.940
- Though the speculators
didn't see this coming,

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.090
some financiers did.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.600
A handful of savvy investors
predicted the housing crash

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.950
and made a ton of money
betting against the market

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:44.430
in a move that's come to
be known as the Big Short.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.030
One of the most prominent of them

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:49.450
was a Goldman Sachs executive
named Donald Mullen,

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.000
who right after the crash

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.950
pivoted to becoming a speculator himself.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:57.450
- Mullen started an investment
firm called Pretium Partners,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:00.530
which raised over a billion
dollars from foreign investors

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.680
to buy up foreclosed homes across America

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.630
and turn them into rental units.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.040
Today, Pretium is the largest owner

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.030
of single family homes in the country,

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.040
and a lot of other mega corporations

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.450
are following their lead.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.480
- In the competition for limited housing,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.070
these firms enjoy several
massive advantages.

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:22.350
The average American home buyer
will have to find a listing,

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.800
visit the property,
negotiate with the owner,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.060
put in an offer, and secure
a mortgage from a bank,

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:30.280
all before a sale can be made.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.610
If you've ever bought a house,

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.720
you know it can take a while.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.340
- But giant real estate firms like Pretium

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.220
have sophisticated algorithms

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.540
that can scan the market
for the best deals

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.580
and automatically make a cash offer

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:44.520
within hours of the listing.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:46.860
The ease and speed of the transaction

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.550
is so attractive to sellers,

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:50.750
they're even willing to take less money.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:53.660
Researchers estimate that
investors like Pretium

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.890
actually pay around 10% less
than individual home buyers,

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:59.360
thanks to this advantage.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:02.050
- They tend to target
majority black neighborhoods

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:06.180
at a rate almost three times
higher than other zip codes.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.070
Why?

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:10.670
It may be because their
algorithms perceive these houses

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:13.030
as artificially undervalued.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.770
In other words, prejudice
keeps prices lower

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:17.320
than what they should be.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.650
If the neighborhood can be gentrified,

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:23.810
there's a potential for
a larger profit margin.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.440
- Pretium's property
management subsidiary,

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:27.810
Progress Residential,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.890
doesn't have many satisfied customers.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:32.350
Families that rent from them

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.180
complain about the shoddy maintenance

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.210
and ever-increasing rents and fees,

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.660
and they are far more
aggressive about evictions

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:40.420
than mom and pop landlords.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:43.097
One housing advocate
called their standard lease

00:04:43.097 --> 00:04:45.740
"about as punitive as we've seen..."

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:47.790
- Defenders of such corporate landlords

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.670
argue that they offer single
family residence living

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:54.170
to many people who'd otherwise
never be able to achieve it.

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.620
They also claim that private equity firms

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:58.520
buying up foreclosed homes

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.840
is what helped the housing
market recover from the collapse.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.240
And does it really matter

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.550
whether a family is
paying rent or a mortgage?

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:10.810
After all, "the reality is
most people don't own a home.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.770
They rent the home from the bank.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:15.550
From the outside, it
really looks the same."

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:17.670
- That quote was said by Pretium's

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.990
head of real estate, Dana Hamilton,

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:21.490
who is definitely smart enough

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:22.820
to know the enormous difference

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:25.890
between renting and owning: equity.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:29.730
For 80 years, home ownership
has been the number one way

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.720
for ordinary Americans
to save for the future

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:35.380
and start creating generational wealth.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.500
- That's what makes real estate attractive

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:38.970
to firms like Pretium,

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.940
which is why some housing advocates

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:44.010
call their business
practice equity-mining,

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.300
stripping neighborhoods of the
pool of investment potential

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:50.570
that used to be accessible
to the general population.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:53.390
- Well, there are other ways
to build equity, you might say.

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:56.620
In fact, the stock market has
seen overall better returns

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:59.210
than real estate over the last 80 years.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:02.550
True, but you can't
live in your portfolio.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:05.070
A house is that rare kind of purchase

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:08.330
that doubles as an
investment and a shelter.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.080
- Also, mortgages offer far better terms

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:12.550
than personal loans.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:16.120
No bank is gonna give you
$200,000 at 4% interest

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:18.490
so you can go buy a bunch of stock.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:21.810
They'd get a better return just
buying the stock themselves.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:24.860
The government deliberately
started the mortgage program

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.390
to offer ordinary people
the chance to build equity.

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:31.220
It's widely credited with
creating the middle class,

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:35.280
and the tacit exclusion of
black Americans from the program

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.160
is why today they suffer

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:40.550
from a vast discrepancy
in generational wealth.

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:43.220
- Now, we are not saying
there's anything wrong

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:44.630
with investing in real estate.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:46.630
In fact, we've often recommended it

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:49.160
as a reliable way to grow your money,

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:50.520
but we're not talking about someone

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:52.860
taking out a loan to buy a rental property

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:54.450
to save for retirement.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:57.950
This is a corporate-backed
takeover of America's suburbs

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:01.070
at a time when housing is in short supply.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:02.320
- The government could try

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.200
to discourage the trend through taxation,

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:08.100
but rent is not considered capital gains.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:12.160
And companies like Pretium are
run through offshore havens,

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:15.360
protecting them from most U.S. taxes.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:16.240
At the end of the day,

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:18.540
the government can't really tell someone

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:21.690
who they can or can't sell their house to.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:25.410
- Many housing advocates concede
that the most hopeful path

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.940
to making home ownership accessible again

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:31.420
is to simply build more houses and fast.

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:34.300
Increasing the inventory
of available homes

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:35.530
will lower prices

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:38.850
and make the market less
attractive to speculators.

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:39.990
Many states are currently

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:42.970
changing their zoning
laws to speed this up.

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:46.090
- In the meantime, if you're
one of the unfortunate buyers

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:49.430
competing with mega firms,
don't get discouraged.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:51.420
It may take more time and effort,

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:53.820
but owning a home is still worth it.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.220
And unlike the last price increase,

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:59.340
we're far less likely to
see a massive bubble burst

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.360
that will leave homeowners
holding the bag.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:05.150
- Also, renting doesn't have
to be a dead-end street.

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:07.340
As we've explained in a previous video,

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:09.050
there are some advantages,

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:10.560
as long as you find other ways

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.400
to build equity and save money,

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.270
so you can possibly have a down payment

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:16.900
when you're ready to
enter the housing market.

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:18.600
- [Both] And that's our two cents.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:23.470
- If you're worried about
being stuck renting,

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:26.998
you might wanna check out our
video, "Is It Okay to Rent?".

00:08:26.998 --> 00:08:29.665
(upbeat music)

